  "live_records": {
    "q-163773": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163773",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ what type of point is $a?$",
      "worked_solution": "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
