    "q-163795": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163795",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14206588/assets/a39696fcb202485b9a58839609ad807e43397fde8df965bed2824f6cdc307703.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=1.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ and  $(x,y)=(1,0),$so the global maximum points are $(-1,-2,1)$ and $(1,0,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14206588/assets/4a818d3e3e3c6c6a90ff9e69d808803016114894fbc605de5d21c75817eb8cf3.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-163795-a",
