    "q-164101": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164101",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14206588/assets/f4ca398cfb365346962fc66ffecd7fb85390a9a98c49cb1926f6d8093a01c8bc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?",
      "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence, $A$ and $C$ are local minima.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14206588/assets/0262a775dad79697ea888b6047e607c12ec804dbb734b72b2ca8fda54d6c130f.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-164101-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "$A,$ $C,$ and $D$ only",
